## 7. Tables
Use XML-style table markup when block attributes, colors, or explicit table configuration are needed.

```text
<table fit-page-width="true" header-row="true" header-column="false">
	<colgroup>
		<col color="gray">
		<col color="blue_bg">
	</colgroup>
	<tr color="purple_bg">
		<td>**Field**</td>
		<td>**Value**</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>Status</td>
		<td><span color="green">Approved</span></td>
	</tr>
</table>
```

Rules:

- `fit-page-width`, `header-row`, and `header-column` accept `true` or `false`.
- All three attributes are optional and default to `false`.
- Table cells may contain rich text only.
- Do not place headings, lists, callouts, nested tables, or other block types inside `<td>`.
- Color precedence is: cell → row → column.
- Do not use undocumented column-width attributes.
- Programmatic cell merging is not supported through enhanced markdown.

When a table is simple and requires no attributes, standard Markdown table syntax may also be accepted:

```text
| Status | Owner |
|---|---|
| In progress | Ada |
```

For BMAD documents, prefer tables for compact comparisons, traceability matrices, role mappings, risk registers, and acceptance-criteria summaries. Avoid tables for long narrative content.

