## 11. Mentions, references, and citations
### 11.1 Mentions

```text
<mention-user url="URL">User name</mention-user>
<mention-page url="URL">Page title</mention-page>
<mention-database url="URL">Database name</mention-database>
<mention-data-source url="URL">Data source name</mention-data-source>
<mention-agent url="URL">Agent name</mention-agent>
<mention-date start="2026-07-11"/>
<mention-date start="2026-07-11" end="2026-07-15"/>
<mention-date start="2026-07-11" startTime="20:00" timeZone="Asia/Colombo"/>
```

Self-closing mention forms are permitted when the backend can resolve display text:

```text
<mention-page url="URL"/>
```

### 11.2 Citations

```text
[^https://example.com/source]
```

A citation contains a URL inside `[^...]`.

Do not use compressed placeholders such as `[^{{1}}]` unless the consuming system explicitly resolves them before sending the content to Notion.
### 11.3 Child page and database blocks

```text
<page url="URL" color="gray">Page title</page>
<database url="URL" inline="false" icon="📚" color="gray">Database title</database>
```

These are structural child-page or child-database blocks, not ordinary hyperlinks.

Use inline mention tags when the intent is only to reference an existing object:

```text
See <mention-page url="URL">Architecture decision record</mention-page>.
```

---

## 12. Synced blocks

```text
<synced_block>
	Shared content
</synced_block>
```

Reference an existing synced block only when a valid block URL is available:

```text
<synced_block_reference url="URL">
	Mirrored content
</synced_block_reference>
```

Do not fabricate synced-block URLs.

Do not assume that removing a synced-block representation from a generated document is harmless. Fetch the current page and use targeted updates whenever preserving existing structure matters.

---

## 13. Table of contents

```text
<table_of_contents color="gray"/>
```

Use a table of contents for long BMAD documents with multiple second-level sections.

Place it near the top of the page, after the executive summary or metadata callout.

---

## 14. Meeting notes
A meeting-notes block may appear in content retrieved from Notion:

```text
<meeting-notes>
	Meeting title
	<notes>
		- Captured note
	</notes>
</meeting-notes>
```

Treat meeting-note transcript and generated-summary content as system-managed.

Important behaviors:

- Transcripts are omitted by default when reading markdown.
- They are included only when the fetch operation requests `include_transcript=true`.
- Update matching does not operate across transcript text.
- Do not attempt to manufacture or overwrite transcript content.
- Avoid using transcript content as a search-and-replace anchor.

For ordinary BMAD meeting summaries, create normal headings, paragraphs, lists, and action items instead of synthesizing a native meeting-notes container.

---

## 15. Unsupported and unknown blocks
The enhanced-markdown API may return:

```text
<unknown url="URL" alt="block_type"/>
```

Common unsupported types include:
- bookmarks
- third-party embeds
- link previews
- breadcrumbs
- deprecated template buttons

Never silently delete an `<unknown>` block.

When updating an existing page:
1. fetch the latest page content
2. preserve unknown blocks unless deletion is explicitly intended
3. use targeted updates rather than replacing the entire page
4. re-fetch and verify the result
Large or partially accessible pages may also return unknown blocks because of truncation or permissions.

