## 16. Safe creation and update behavior
### 16.1 Create
When creating a page from enhanced markdown:

- use the markdown field instead of block children
- do not send markdown and children/content in the same request
- provide a page title explicitly when possible
- for large pages, use asynchronous creation when supported
### 16.2 Update
Prefer targeted search-and-replace updates when changing an existing document.

Each search string should match exactly one location unless replacing all matches is intentional.

Use full-content replacement only when:
- the entire page is owned by the generator
- all existing child structures have been inspected
- unknown blocks have been handled
- destructive replacement is explicitly intended
### 16.3 Child-page and child-database protection
Current markdown update operations protect child pages and child databases by default.

If an update would delete them, Notion returns a validation error unless deletion is explicitly authorized through the update command.

Therefore:
- do not claim that omission automatically and silently deletes children
- do treat full-content replacement as potentially destructive
- never enable deletion merely to bypass a validation error
- use `<mention-page>` or `<mention-database>` for references rather than structural child blocks
### 16.4 Verification after mutation
After every create or update:
1. inspect the returned markdown or fetch the page again
2. confirm the expected heading hierarchy
3. confirm lists and nested blocks remained nested
4. check for `<unknown>` blocks
5. confirm child pages and databases still exist
6. confirm Mermaid and tables rendered correctly
7. record any lossy conversion or unsupported content
